>   The only stupid questions I have for now - very pressing - how do you save g
>ames to restore them if you get killed?  I play on a PC, and I can exit the gam
>e and copy the save file in DOS, but I figure there has to be an easier way.

No, there is no easier way. If it were possible, even this way would
have been made impossible, because it is considered cheating. When you
weren't playing on a PC but on, for example, a UNIX system, you
wouldn't be able to copy your save files at all...

Unlike other adventure games, NetHack is different each time you play
it, so there is no good excuse to restart a game when you get killed.
Other games usually are the same each time you play them, so it would
be tedious to play the same dungeon over and over again.

NetHack on the contrary is different with each game. Thus, it is
considered cheating and unsportsmanlike to copy saved games and use
them to restart a game when you have simply LOST. Those that get killed
are no losers: those that use backup save files to restart a game when
they have been killed are... The only "accepted" use of save file
copying is using a save file when you have a system crash. If this is
the case, however, you can usually use the "recover" program to recover
your game.

